  "summary": "Preprints and publications this month apply cell-cycle, agent-based, and mechano-signaling models to tissue-scale questions in development, culture, and injury.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is quantitative modelling of how mechanical and cell-cycle processes shape tissue-scale organization. In the Drosophila wing epithelium, a cell-cycle model coupled to tissue mechanics, parameterized from wing disc time-lapse imaging, reproduces cell area distributions. A review of Deformable Cell Models describes high-resolution agent-based simulation of cell biophysics with in-vitro applications. A spatial proteomics study infers a dynamical model of cell population change from a single tissue sample, linking neighbourhood composition to division rate. Separately, a mechano-signaling model links shear-induced chondrocyte damage to MMP release and depth-wise collagen loss in cartilage. What is new is inference of temporal dynamics from a single spatial snapshot and explicit coupling of mechanical injury to matrix degradation. The items vary in scope; the shared element is mechanistic simulation constrained by measured tissue properties.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-01-21"
}
